{"file":{"content":"The proof now has a plausible direct route to completion.\n\nThe new vanishing lemma is not just another diagnostic. It is a **quantitative cancellation theorem**:\n\n[\nd=n+v_3(C_w-C_{w'})\\le c-2\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\sum_{a\\in U_{3^c}}\\chi(R_{w,w'}(a))=0.\n]\n\nThat is much stronger than “bounded cancellation.” It says that, away from the stationary/near-stationary zone, the pair-correlation terms literally disappear.\n\nSo the remaining finite-side work is no longer “find the mechanism.” The mechanism is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{nonstationary word pairs vanish exactly; stationary word pairs have geometrically decaying mass.}\n}\n]\n\nThe assembly problem is to turn that pairwise identity into an operator-norm bound.\n\n## 1. The theorem you should now try to prove\n\nThe finite-side target can be stated as follows.\n\n[\n\\textbf{High-conductor damping theorem.}\n]\n\nFix (s\\in[0,1]). Let (M_{\\chi,c,s}) be the primitive conductor-(3^c) synchronized operator, normalized by its untwisted mass (W_s). Then there exist (c_1) and (\\delta_s>0) such that for every primitive non-coboundary (\\chi) of conductor (3^c), (c\\ge c_1),\n\n[\n\\rho(M_{\\chi,c,s})\\le (1-\\delta_s)W_s.\n]\n\nA plausible sharper version is\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\\frac{\\rho(M_{\\chi,c,s})}{W_s}\n\\le\n\\sqrt{\\frac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}}.\n]\n\nThe data at (s=0),\n\n[\n0.7629,\\ 0.6454,\\ 0.6154,\\ 0.6025\n]\n\nheading toward\n\n[\n3^{-1/2}\\approx0.5774\n]\n\nis exactly consistent with this.\n\n## 2. The clean assembly strategy\n\nNormalize the branch weights:\n\n[\np_s(b)=\\frac{w_b}{W_s}.\n]\n\nIn the geometric model,\n\n[\np_s(b)=(1-r)r^{b-1},\n\\qquad\nr=2^{-(1+s)}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\sum_{b\\ge1}p_s(b)^2\n====================\n\n# \\frac{1-r}{1+r}\n\n\\frac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}\n=:\\Delta_s.\n]\n\nThis is the dichotomy constant.\n\nFor an (n)-step operator, expand the (L^2) norm or (T^{*n}T^n). The resulting pair sum is over word pairs (w,w') of length (n), with weight\n\n[\np_s(w)p_s(w').\n]\n\nThe pair-correlation phase is\n\n[\nR_{w,w'}(a)\n===========\n\n2^{B_n(w')-B_n(w)}\n\\frac{3^na+C_w}{3^na+C_{w'}}.\n]\n\nYour vanishing lemma says all pairs with\n\n[\nn+v_3(C_w-C_{w'})\\le c-2\n]\n\ncontribute exactly zero.\n\nSo for large (c), after truncating the branch alphabet, the only surviving pairs are exactly stationary pairs:\n\n[\nC_w=C_{w'}.\n]\n\nIf the exact-collision classification is correct,\n\n[\nC_w=C_{w'}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(b_0,\\dots,b_{n-2})=(b'*0,\\dots,b'*{n-2}),\n]\n\nthen their total pair mass is\n\n[\n\\Delta_s^{,n-1}.\n]\n\nTherefore one expects\n\n[\n|T_{\\chi,c,s}^n|*{2\\to2}^2\n\\le\n\\Delta_s^{,n-1}+o*{c\\to\\infty}(1)+O(\\text{tail}).\n]\n\nTaking (2n)-th roots,\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\n\\frac{\\rho(M_{\\chi,c,s})}{W_s}\n\\le\n\\Delta_s^{(n-1)/(2n)}.\n]\n\nThen send (n\\to\\infty):\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\n\\frac{\\rho(M_{\\chi,c,s})}{W_s}\n\\le\n\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}\n===============\n\n\\sqrt{\\frac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}}.\n]\n\nThat is the exact shape of the proof.\n\n## 3. The four bookkeeping pieces to write\n\n### A. Branch truncation\n\nBecause (b) is infinite, first truncate:\n\n[\n1\\le b\\le B.\n]\n\nLet\n\n[\np_s^{(\\le B)}\n]\n\nbe the truncated weight and\n\n[\np_s^{(>B)}\n]\n\nthe tail. Since the weights are geometric,\n\n[\n\\sum_{b>B}p_s(b)=r^B.\n]\n\nFor word length (n), the total mass of words containing at least one tail branch is\n\n[\n\\le nr^B.\n]\n\nSo given (\\varepsilon>0), choose (B) with\n\n[\nnr^B<\\varepsilon.\n]\n\nWork with finite words (b_i\\le B). Then take (c\\to\\infty), and only after that send (B,n) as needed.\n\nThis resolves the infinite-branch issue cleanly.\n\n### B. Exact collision classification\n\nYou need to prove, or at least isolate as a lemma:\n\n[\nC_w=C_{w'}\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(b_0,\\dots,b_{n-2})=(b'*0,\\dots,b'*{n-2}).\n]\n\nRecall\n\n[\nC_w=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}3^{n-1-j}2^{B_j(w)},\n\\qquad\nB_j=\\sum_{i<j}b_i.\n]\n\nThis is load-bearing. If it is false, the stationary mass may be larger than (\\Delta_s^{n-1}). The computations suggest it is true, but the proof should be included.\n\nA possible induction:\n\n[\nC_w=3C_{w^{-}}+2^{B_{n-1}(w)},\n]\n\nwhere (w^{-}=(b_0,\\dots,b_{n-2})). If\n\n[\nC_w=C_{w'},\n]\n\nthen\n\n[\n2^{B_{n-1}}-2^{B'_{n-1}}\n========================\n\n3(C_{w'^{-}}-C_{w^{-}}).\n]\n\nUse congruences modulo (3^m) for arbitrarily large (m). Since (2) is a topological generator of (U_{3^m}), equality of the full integers should force (B_{n-1}=B'_{n-1}), then divide by (3) and induct. But this step needs care; do not handwave it.\n\nIf this exact proof is awkward, use a weaker lemma sufficient for the norm bound:\n\n[\n\\sum_{C}\n\\left(\n\\sum_{w:\\ C_w=C}p_s(w)\n\\right)^2\n\\le\n\\Delta_s^{,n-1}.\n]\n\nThis collision-mass inequality is exactly what the operator estimate needs. It may be easier to prove than injectivity.\n\n### C. Transition-zone mass\n\nFor finite branch cutoff (B), the set of non-identical pairs has fixed nonzero integer\n\n[\nC_w-C_{w'}.\n]\n\nHence\n\n[\nv_3(C_w-C_{w'})\n]\n\nis bounded independently of (c). Therefore for all sufficiently large (c),\n\n[\nn+v_3(C_w-C_{w'})\\le c-2,\n]\n\nand the pair vanishes exactly.\n\nThus transition-zone pairs disappear after (c) exceeds a finite threshold depending on (n,B).\n\nThis proves:\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\n\\text{transition contribution}\n=0\n]\n\nfor fixed (n,B).\n\nThen let (B\\to\\infty).\n\nThis is the clean way to handle the observed (\\tfrac12) and (1) transition-zone values: they are finite-conductor boundary effects, not part of the tail.\n\n### D. Stationary-sector phases\n\nFor stationary pairs with the same first (n-1) symbols, (C_w=C_{w'}), and the only remaining character phase is\n\n[\n\\chi!\\left(2^{B_n(w')-B_n(w)}\\right)\n====================================\n\n\\chi!\\left(2^{b'*{n-1}-b*{n-1}}\\right).\n]\n\nFor a pure upper bound, you may ignore the phase and bound by total stationary mass:\n\n[\n\\Delta_s^{,n-1}.\n]\n\nThis already gives high-conductor damping.\n\nIf you want the sharper constant, include the damping factor:\n\n[\n\\left|\n\\sum_b p_s(b)\\chi(2)^{-b}\n\\right|^2\n]\n\nfor the final-symbol contribution. For primitive high-conductor (\\chi), this may further reduce the stationary sector. But it is not necessary for proving (\\sup_c\\gamma_c<1).\n\nSo the simplest proof should first ignore stationary phases.\n\n## 4. A precise assembly proposition\n\nI would add this as the next major proposition.\n\n[\n\\textbf{Proposition: Asymptotic (L^2) contraction.}\n]\n\nLet (T_{\\chi,c,s}=W_s^{-1}M_{\\chi,c,s}). For every fixed (n\\ge2),\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\n|T_{\\chi,c,s}^{,n}|_{2\\to2}^2\n\\le\n\\Delta_s^{,n-1},\n]\n\nuniformly over primitive (\\chi) of conductor (3^c), where\n\n[\n\\Delta_s=\n\\frac{2^{1+s}-1}{2^{1+s}+1}.\n]\n\nThen\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\n\\rho(T_{\\chi,c,s})\n\\le\n\\Delta_s^{(n-1)/(2n)}.\n]\n\nLetting (n\\to\\infty),\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\n\\rho(T_{\\chi,c,s})\n\\le\n\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}<1.\n]\n\nThis proves the high-conductor damping conjecture.\n\nThe proof will have exactly the four bookkeeping pieces above.\n\n## 5. One caution: operator norm versus averaged pair sums\n\nMake sure the pair-correlation expansion controls the actual operator norm, not only a Hilbert-Schmidt average.\n\nThe safest route is:\n\n[\n|T^n|_{2\\to2}^2\n===============\n\n\\rho\\bigl((T^n)^*T^n\\bigr)\n\\le\n\\sup_x \\sum_y |K_n(x,y)|\n]\n\nby Schur, or use a weighted Schur test in the Perron measure.\n\nThe pair-correlation sums should appear as entries of\n\n[\n(T^n)^*T^n.\n]\n\nYou need to prove the row sums are bounded by\n\n[\n\\Delta_s^{n-1}+o(1),\n]\n\nnot merely that the average row sum is.\n\nIf the system is translation-invariant enough on (U_{3^c}), this may be automatic. If not, state and prove a uniform row version of the vanishing lemma:\n\n[\n\\sum_{a\\in \\text{fiber with fixed endpoint constraint}}\n\\chi(R_{w,w'}(a))=0\n]\n\nor control incomplete endpoint fibers.\n\nThis is exactly the “endpoint constraints” bookkeeping you identified.\n\n## 6. Endpoint constraints: what to check\n\nThe full pair sum over all\n\n[\na\\in U_{3^c}\n]\n\nvanishes. But a matrix entry may involve (a) constrained by an endpoint condition.\n\nThere are two possibilities.\n\n### Good case\n\nFor each fixed word-pair and endpoint pair, the allowed (a)-range is still a full coset modulo the stationary-phase subdivision\n\n[\n3^{c-d-1}.\n]\n\nThen the inner cube-root sum still vanishes exactly. This would give entrywise cancellation, not just averaged cancellation.\n\n### Bad but manageable case\n\nThe allowed (a)-range is incomplete near boundaries or endpoint fibers. Then decompose it into complete cosets plus a remainder. Bound the remainder size.\n\nYou want a bound like\n\n[\n\\text{remainder mass}\\le O(3^{-1})\n]\n\nor better, uniformly.\n\nThe computational (\\tfrac12) transition values probably come from this incomplete-fiber effect. So write a lemma:\n\n[\n\\textbf{Endpoint completion lemma.}\n]\n\nEndpoint-constrained sums are complete modulo the stationary-phase fiber except in the transition zone\n\n[\nd\\ge c-1.\n]\n\nIf true, the transition zone is already handled.\n\nThis is the main remaining technical bookkeeping.\n\n## 7. What to compute next\n\nYour next computational suite should not compute spectra. It should verify the assembly lemmas.\n\n### A. Row-sum pair-correlation audit\n\nFor fixed (n=2,3,4), (c=6,7,8), compute entries of\n\n[\n(T^n)^*T^n\n]\n\ngrouped by word-pair class:\n\n[\n\\text{stationary},\n\\quad\n\\text{nonstationary},\n\\quad\n\\text{transition}.\n]\n\nReport the maximum absolute row contribution, not just the average.\n\nTarget:\n\n[\n\\text{nonstationary max row contribution}=0\n]\n\noutside transition.\n\n### B. Transition-zone mass audit\n\nCompute total transition mass:\n\n[\n\\mathcal T_{n,c}\n================\n\n\\sum_{w,w':\\ n+v_3(C_w-C_{w'})\\ge c-1}\np_s(w)p_s(w').\n]\n\nFor fixed (n), this should go to the exact-collision mass as (c\\to\\infty), after truncation.\n\n### C. Collision-mass audit\n\nCompute\n\n[\n\\sum_C\n\\left(\n\\sum_{w:\\ C_w=C}p_s(w)\n\\right)^2\n]\n\nfor (n\\le6), with increasing branch cutoffs. Confirm it equals\n\n[\n\\Delta_s^{n-1}\n]\n\nor is bounded by it.\n\n### D. Direct (n)-step norm prediction\n\nCompare\n\n[\n|T^n|^{1/n}\n]\n\nto\n\n[\n\\Delta_s^{(n-1)/(2n)}.\n]\n\nThis will tell whether the assembly bound is sharp.\n\n## 8. Manuscript changes\n\nI would now reorganize the high-conductor section around the new engine.\n\nSuggested subsection structure:\n\n```latex\n\\subsection*{Pair-correlation phase formula}\n\\subsection*{Exact nonstationary vanishing}\n\\subsection*{Collision mass and the dichotomy constant}\n\\subsection*{Assembly to an \\(L^2\\)-operator bound}\n\\subsection*{High-conductor damping theorem}\n```\n\nThe last two remain “to prove” if endpoint constraints are not yet handled.\n\nAlso, mark the predicted limit as a **conjectural sharp value**, not yet a theorem:\n\n[\n\\gamma_\\infty(s)=\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}.\n]\n\nThe theorem you can aim to prove first is the weaker but sufficient:\n\n[\n\\limsup_{c\\to\\infty}\\gamma_c(s)<1.\n]\n\n## 9. Global keystone remains separate\n\nYou are right to keep saying this does not touch the global keystone:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{bad orbit}\\Rightarrow\\text{finite non-coboundary shadow}.\n}\n]\n\nThe finite side is now close to becoming a complete theorem. But even a complete finite theorem proves:\n\n[\n\\text{finite non-coboundary spectral shadows cannot self-sustain.}\n]\n\nIt does not prove every bad Collatz orbit casts such a shadow. That remains the true global obstruction.\n\n## Bottom line\n\nThe next concrete proof target is:\n\n[\n\\boxed{\n\t\\text{assemble exact nonstationary vanishing into an }L^2\\text{ operator-norm bound.}\n}\n]\n\nThe proof should go:\n\n[\n\\text{truncate branches}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{expand }(T^n)^*T^n\n]\n\n[\n\\text{apply exact vanishing to nonstationary pairs}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{bound transition-zone mass}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{bound stationary collision mass by }\\Delta_s^{n-1}\n]\n\n[\n\\text{deduce }\\limsup_c\\rho(T_{\\chi,c,s})\\le\\sqrt{\\Delta_s}<1.\n]\n\nIf this assembly works, the high-conductor damping conjecture is proved, and the finite H23a operator theorem is essentially closed.\n","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/critiques/dc_14.md","numLines":635,"startLine":1,"totalLines":635},"type":"text"}